  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1138 AM MDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nNo real changes to model guidance for the short term. Upper high\ncurrently over NM will be shifting to the west over the next 24-48\nhours with upper flow turning more NNE and allowing for some\ndisturbances and a weak frontal boundary to move into the CWA.\nLater this afternoon and tonight there will be a little more \nconvection that yesterday, but will remained focused along and \nwest of the Divide and in the Sacs. Overnight lows will again be\nmild with most of the lowlands around 75-80 degrees again. \n\nFor Fri/Sat, a more active pattern sets up with a N to E flow \ndeveloping. At the same time a slow moving upper low will be\nmoving through the Upper Mississippi Valley which will bring a\nweak frontal boundary in from the NE. This boundary won't make it\ninto the lowlands until late Fri which will allow temperatures to\nwarm up into the 100-110 range. It will be close to the hottest\nday of the summer for many areas. Expanded the Heat Advisory\nthroughout the lowlands as well as the foothills and valley\nlocations in the Gila region. As the front moves through, there\nwill be storms developing over the mountains and then pushing\nstorms onto the lowlands due to outflows. Moisture will be near\naverage with PW's in the 1-1.2\" range with weak shear and\ninstability so expecting more popcorn type storms to develop along\nthe outflows. Brief moderate/heavy rain is possible. For Saturday,\nthere will be a more shear/instability as well as moisture. This\nlooks like the main day to focus on with a potential for some\nsevere storms really anywhere but think parameters line up best\neast of the RGV. Temperatures here will be about 5-10 degrees\ncooler. \n\nDrier air starts to push in from the NE as early as Sunday with\nstorms being much more isolated and mainly in the mountains. This\nalso looks like the \"coolest\" day with most areas not getting\nabove 100 degrees. \n\nFor next week, an elongated upper high becomes established across\nthe southern US. The strength is still in question, but both the\nEC/GFS and their ensemble means support this and with a generally\neasterly mid/upper level flow over the area, Gulf moisture stays\ngenerally south and west bringing us back to the pattern we have\nrecently seen as storms remain focused over the Gila region and\nwest of the Divide. Temperatures will also climb back into the\nupper 90s to around 105 for the lowlands.",
